rds the potentially opening  of this coal mine  I think it is and and the key sentence  he mentioned was wait and see what  happens with the gold mine because  um the Secretary of State might have  approved it but I think we still have a  long way to go before actually digging  the coal out of the ground in Cumbria uh  but the bigger picture of course is  really looking at the fact that we still  do not have our institutions and our  policies and our laws in line with Net  Zero uh the fact that a local Authority  for example uh on the basis of jobs on  the basis of a few other things that  might just benefit uh a few people in a  local area which is also a doubt uh you  know is Up For Debate uh can can  undermine sort of the Net Zero  legislation altogether is is up for the  bit is he is concerned so what we really  need to think about is how do we align a  lot of our institutional mechanisms  policies and laws in line with what  we've got with Net Zero and Mr Kumar one  of the more prominent aspects of the  review was talking about how Net Zero  can actually help boost the economy did  you think that was quite encouraging  it was and I'd argue that was one of the  highlights of the review uh if you  consider the background of this review  it was commissioned by the former prime  minister Liz truss and some of our  cabinet members really had a doubt  whether netsuda was a good thing or  perhaps we're going too fast on Net Zero  and that's why they commissioned this  review and the review comes out very  clear it's fairly unambiguous in stating  the biggest Economic Opportunity for  Britain in the 21st century is in  NetZero is really an inviting investment  for the low-carbon economy of the future  and it's also on sort of addressing some  of the bigger challenges the other  challenges that the government has  identified like leveling up uh the fact  that parts of the knob are left behind  in many ways Net Zero is another  opportunity another Avenue to try and  lift those regions and improve the  economy of those regions as well so I  think it's a overall a very very  positive report and it's not just the  review from one person it's not the  opinion of one person about 1800  businesses and Civil Society members  have submitted their views so it's an  overwhelming opinion of a majority of  the population here that's essentially  saying we need to go faster than that  zero because that's where the  opportunity for economy and well-being  really lie  okay try telling uh Kumar thank you very  much for your time on the daily climate  show  okay let's take a look at some other  stories making news today for you and  climate activates have occupied a giant  Digger at a coal mine in Western Germany  it's all part of that ongoing protest  against the destruction of a nearby  Village for the expansion of a separate  mine our Energy company rwe said that  four people climbed onto a digger early  this morning and operations at lignite  mine or they've been forced  more than 50 heads of state  International organizations and Business  Leaders a meeting in the Swiss Alpine  Resort of davros this week for the 2023  meeting of the world economic Forum with  discussions on the program heavily  linked to climate change now some  activists have raised concerns about  greenwashing at the summit and  campaigners including Vanessa nacarte  and Greta tunberg will travel to the  resort in the coming days bringing a  cease and desist letter to oil and gas  CEOs demanding they stop any new oil and  gas projects  now the U.S climate Envoy has backed the  United Arab Emirates decision to appoint  the CEO of a state-run oil company to  preside over the upcoming U.N climate  negotiations in Dubai or John Kerry said  Sultan our Jabba's appointment was a  terrific Choice sightseeing his work on  renewable energy projects  I think that Dr Sultan al-jabbar is a  terrific choice because he is the head  of the company  that company knows it needs to  transition he knows and the leadership  of the UAE is committed to transitioning  and in fact the UA is one of the largest  investors in renewable energy  and if you listen to the speech he gave  yesterday the Atlanta Council he made it  absolutely clear we're not moving fast  enough we have to reduce emissions we  have to begin to accelerate this  transition significantly so I have great  confidence that the right issues are  going to be on the table  most 4 000 baby seals have been spotted  along a Five Mile Stretch of coast in  Norfolk this winter a record number it  demonstrates conditions are good enough  to support a healthy Colony PT Ansel  chairman of friends of horsey seals  which works to protect the animals from  disturbance said the population boom  when it's down to the fish as currently  the North Sea is providing enough fish  for thousands of seals  beavers are set to be reintroduced into  an enclosure in Hampshire marking the  first time in 400 years that they've  lived in the country in Southern England  now a pair of the semi-aquatic mammals  will be released to euhurst park estate  which is being restored for nature and  sustainable food production  now storms in California continued over  the weekend bringing renewed fears of  flooding and mudslides after weeks of  heavy rainfall more than 19 people have  died and flood watches remain in place  for around 8 million people now the  latest storm is expected to bring up to  three feet of snow in Sierra Nevada and  back-to-back atmospheric Rivers have  been blamed for huge floods but what  exactly are they well I'm joined Now by  The Royal meteorological society's chief  executive Professor Liz Bentley Liz  thanks so much for joining us this  afternoon  good afternoon  um so just let us know what are these  atmospheric rivers and why do they in  some cases cause so much trouble  yeah so these are narrow bounds of uh  areas high up in the atmosphere that  hold more water vapor more moisture in  the atmosphere they're effectively  rivers in the sky and because there's  more moisture there more water vapor it  means that when they reach land and they  form a cloud we find that there's extra  amounts of rainfall that fall from these  atmospheric Rivers the normal weather  systems so it's a continuous feed a  conveyor belt I guess of moisture coming  right uh transported long distances  they're very large scale features that  you can see from space satellites can  pick them up particularly those  satellites that just look at water vapor  themselves and uh Liz as the climate  intensifies and heats up can we expect  then these atmospheric rivers to become  more dangerous  yeah so as our climate warms we know  that the atmosphere can hold more  moisture so a one degree warming in  global average temperatures leads to  about a seven percent increase in the  amount of moisture that it can hold so  we expect these atmospheric rivers to be  able to hold more moisture in a warmer  atmosphere than they would normally do  prior to human induced climate change so  they're going to become more active  there's more moisture there therefore  we're going to see heavier rainfall  events and that's been played out  obviously across California at the  moment with the extreme rainfall that  we're seeing so you know it really very  much is a signature of what our climate  is starting to look like and we'll  continue to look like going forward in  time let's talk to me about adaptation  in this area for a moment because I know  that forecasting of these atmospheric  Rivers they can be what about five to  seven days in advance  yeah they're very large scale systems so  they our computer models that look at  forecasts a few days or a few weeks  ahead pick these systems up quite nicely  and then you have the local detail of  where that rain is going to fall or snow  in the case of of uh the rain that's  falling over the higher ground it fall  falls in the form of snow  um and so we can predict these things  well in advance and put warnings in  place so part of I guess the the  adaptation is to get early warnings out  we can't stop the rain from falling but  we can warn people to be able to to you  know take precautions during these heavy  rainfall events other adaptation things  we really need to look at how we uh yeah  our Urban Development so the materials  we use within towns and cities uh aren't  poor of us they don't allow that  moisture that rainfall to soak into the  ground it just forms more localized  flooding so we'll need to look at how we  adapt in towns and cities but also  across rural areas we need to change our  agricultural practices so that we we  allow the soil able to become more  adaptable to hold on to that moisture we  don't take down forests for example  because the the trees themselves can  hold the moisture and we support areas  of wetlands and peatlands which again  access sponge to to store that moisture  so we there's things we can definitely  do with our kind of land use our Urban  Development to to help to adapt to the  the extreme rainfall that we're already  seeing playing out across across parts  of the world and let's just briefly if  we look closer to home you will know  more than anyone you know we've got  forecast a lot of rain previously and  coming up in the next few days can we  expect here in the UK to experience more  of these types of rivers  um closer to home  yeah we have experienced atmospheric  rivers in the UK they come all the way  from the tropical regions like the  Azores and it pumps all that moisture up  to the UK and then it falls in the form  of rain we have weather systems as well  that coming off the Atlantic deep areas  of low so we kind of get hit by by both  effects I guess the atmospheric Rivers  but also our weather weather systems  developing off the Atlantic and again  we're looking really to when the next  weather systems are going to come in  because the ground's already saturated  and any extra rainfalling on it will  lead to further flooding okay Liz  Bentley really interesting to talk to  you thanks for your time on the daily  climate show today  well that's all from us today but don't  worry we're back tomorrow with a whole  lot more see you soon bye

